---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    question: 'How close should my speed test be to my plan?',
    answer:
      'This guide uses 80% of the advertised download speed as its own quick-check rule of thumb. It is not a provider guarantee or a regulatory standard. Repeat the test under fair conditions before drawing a conclusion.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does one low speed test mean my provider is not delivering?',
    answer:
      'No. A single test is a snapshot of one device, place, and moment. Repeat on a wired device near the router at different times and look for a consistent pattern.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How can I test my internet speed fairly?',
    answer:
      'Use a wired device near the router when possible, pause other heavy internet activity, and repeat the test at different times. Keep the device and test method consistent when comparing results.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Guess the Speed send my plan speed anywhere?',
    answer:
      'No. An internet plan entered in Settings is saved in this browser and is never sent to us. The checker compares the plan and measured speed on your device.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: questions.map(({ question, answer }) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: answer },
  })),
};
---

<Base
  title="Am I Getting the Internet Speed I Pay For? A Quick Check"
  description="Compare your internet plan with real download results, test fairly, and learn when a repeated speed gap is worth discussing with your provider."
  breadcrumb="Internet plan check"
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <main class="wrap content-page plan-check-page">
    <span class="round-banner">Speed school</span>
    <h1>Am I Getting the Internet Speed I Pay For? A Quick Check</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Run a speed test on a device close to your router, ideally with a wired
      connection, then compare the download and upload results with your plan.
      Repeat at different times of day. One low result is not proof of a problem,
      but a pattern is a useful reason to investigate.
    </p>

    <section class="plan-check-card" aria-labelledby="plan-check-title">
      <div class="plan-check-intro">
        <span class="plan-check-kicker">A quick download check</span>
        <h2 id="plan-check-title">Compare your plan with a test</h2>
        <p>
          Enter the download speed on your plan and a measured download result.
          The comparison stays in this browser.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="plan-check-fields">
        <label class="plan-check-field">
          Plan download (Mbps)
          <input
            class="dialog-input"
            data-plan-check-plan
            type="number"
            min="1"
            max="10000"
            step="any"
            inputmode="decimal"
            placeholder="500"
          />
        </label>
        <label class="plan-check-field">
          Measured download (Mbps)
          <input
            class="dialog-input"
            data-plan-check-measured
            type="number"
            min="1"
            step="any"
            inputmode="decimal"
            placeholder="420"
          />
        </label>
      </div>
      <output class="plan-check-result" data-plan-check-result aria-live="polite">
        Enter both speeds to see how close your result is.
      </output>
      <div class="plan-check-actions">
        <button class="guess-button" type="button" data-use-last-test>
          Use my last test
        </button>
        <button class="go" type="button" data-save-plan>
          Save as my plan
        </button>
      </div>
      <p class="plan-check-status" data-plan-check-status aria-live="polite"></p>
      <p class="plan-check-note">
        The colors use this site's rule of thumb: 80% or more is close for this
        quick check, 50% to 79% is a gap to keep an eye on, and below 50% is a
        larger gap worth retesting. These bands are not a provider guarantee or
        a regulatory figure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <article class="content-card stk">
      <h2>What counts as close enough?</h2>
      <p>
        For a quick comparison in Guess the Speed, we use 80% of the plan's
        download speed as our own rule of thumb for "close enough." It is a
        practical signal, not a promise about what every device must receive or
        a standard set by a regulator. The percentage helps you notice a gap;
        it cannot explain the cause by itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Providers may describe a service tier as "up to" a speed. A speed test
        measures what reached one device at one moment, and Wi-Fi typically
        delivers less than a wired connection. Compare like with like before
        deciding whether the difference is persistent.
      </p>

      <h2>Why tests come in lower than the plan</h2>
      <p>
        A speed test can be lower for reasons inside the home or along the route
        to the test server. The <a href="/what-is-a-good-internet-speed/#less-than-plan">good internet speed guide explains common causes in more detail</a>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Wi-Fi distance and walls between your device and the router.</li>
        <li>Other people streaming, calling, or downloading during the test.</li>
        <li>An older device or Wi-Fi standard that cannot use the full connection.</li>
        <li>A VPN adding another step between your device and the test.</li>
        <li>The selected test server or the route traffic takes to reach it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to test fairly</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Use a wired connection near the router when one is available.</li>
        <li>Pause large downloads, backups, and other heavy activity for a moment.</li>
        <li>Run the same test more than once, including at different times of day.</li>
        <li>Compare both download and upload with the matching numbers on your plan.</li>
        <li>Keep the device, room, and connection type consistent when repeating.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>When to call your provider</h2>
      <p>
        If several tests across different days stay well below your plan on a
        wired device near the router, keep a short record before contacting
        your provider. Note the date, time, device, connection type, and both
        measured speeds. A repeatable pattern is more useful to discuss than
        one result from one busy evening.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>Your Wi-Fi lately</strong> chart keeps recent real-test
        results in this browser, while Dev mode's JSON download gives you a
        detailed record of a run. Neither includes your saved plan speed.
      </p>

      <h2>Make it a game</h2>
      <p>
        Turn the next test into a family challenge: guess the download and
        upload, run a real test, and see who came closest. <a href="/">Play Guess the Speed</a> or read more about the <a href="/speed-test-game/">speed test game</a>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="content-card stk plan-check-faq">
      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      {questions.map(({ question, answer }) => (
        <section>
          <h3>{question}</h3>
          <p>{answer}</p>
        </section>
      ))}
    </article>
  </main>
  <script>
    import '../scripts/plan-check-client';
  </script>
</Base>

<style>
  .plan-check-card {
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    margin: 0 0 24px;
    padding: clamp(20px, 4vw, 36px);
    border: 3px solid var(--ink);
    border-radius: 20px;
    background: var(--surface);
    box-shadow: 7px 7px 0 var(--shadow);
  }

  .plan-check-intro h2 {
    margin: 5px 0 8px;
    font-size: clamp(26px, 4vw, 36px);
  }

  .plan-check-intro p {
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .plan-check-kicker {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .plan-check-fields {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 14px;
  }

  .plan-check-field {
    display: grid;
    gap: 7px;
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  .plan-check-result {
    display: block;
    padding: 16px 18px;
    border: 2px solid var(--ink);
    border-radius: 14px;
    background: var(--soft);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: clamp(19px, 3vw, 25px);
    font-weight: 900;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  .plan-check-result[data-tone='good'] {
    border-color: var(--on-pastel);
    background: #b9edc8;
    color: var(--on-pastel);
  }

  .plan-check-result[data-tone='ok'] {
    border-color: var(--on-pastel);
    background: #ffe08a;
    color: var(--on-pastel);
  }

  .plan-check-result[data-tone='low'] {
    border-color: var(--on-pastel);
    background: #ffc5bd;
    color: var(--on-pastel);
  }

  .plan-check-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .plan-check-status,
  .plan-check-note {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .plan-check-faq section + section {
    margin-top: 20px;
  }

  .plan-check-faq h3 {
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }

  .plan-check-faq p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .plan-check-fields {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .plan-check-actions > button {
      width: 100%;
    }
  }
</style>
